  "publicationDraft": "Stew sold Lamb while Dallas was sputtering, acquiring Pollard and rookie Smith-Njigba. Contemporary analysts actually endorsed buying Pollard and selling Lamb. Then Lamb went nuclear. Birch got 251.30 observed starting points from Lamb alone, while Stew flipped Pollard in a November three-team deal. Gainwell never started and was cut. Birch beat Stew in Week 14, but lost to Stilly in Week 15; Lamb's spectacular Week 17 outing came in an unpaired lineup. Stew's decision was defensible at the time, but the realized return was brutal. Birch bought the dip and got the damn jackpot.",
